Theorem 1.10 defines \([X]=\{x\in|\mathcal{D}|\mid X\in x\}\) and asserts that these subsets form a neighbourhood system over \(|\mathcal{D}|\) whose domain is isomorphic to \(|\mathcal{D}|\). The written proof gives \([\Delta]=|\mathcal{D}|\), consistency of \(X\) and \(Y\) if and only if \([X]\cap[Y]\neq\emptyset\), the identity \([X]\cap[Y]=[X\cap Y]\) when \(X\cap Y\in\mathcal{D}\), and \(\uparrow X\in[X]\), and then passes from the one-one inclusion-preserving correspondence \(X\mapsto[X]\) to a correspondence of elements. `bracket` is \([X]\). `bracket_master` is the first identity, because every filter contains the master. `bracket_inter_nonempty_iff` is the consistency criterion: a common element contains \(X\cap Y\), which is then a neighbourhood contained in the intersection, and a common lower bound \(Z\) places the principal filter of \(Z\) in both brackets. `bracket_inter` is the intersection identity, by the intersection law one way and by upward closure along \(X\cap Y\subseteq X\) and \(X\cap Y\subseteq Y\) the other, proved for every pair of neighbourhoods and therefore in particular when the intersection is itself a neighbourhood. `principal_mem_bracket` is membership of the principal filter. `bracket_subset_iff` and `bracket_injective` are the inclusion-reflecting bijection of neighbourhoods. `tokenSystem` takes the brackets of neighbourhoods as its neighbourhoods and the set of all elements as master, and it closes a consistent intersection by recovering a common lower bound from the principal filter of the witnessing bracket and returning the bracket of the intersection. `toToken` sends a filter to the filter of brackets of its members, `ofToken` returns the neighbourhoods whose brackets lie in a filter of the token system, and `tokenIso` shows that the two translations are inverse and reflect approximation by injectivity of brackets, so `isomorphic_tokenSystem` is the isomorphism of domains. **Theorem:** yes — the bracket system determines a domain isomorphic to the original domain. **Proof:** yes — the four listed identities and the induced order isomorphism of elements are Scott's argument, with the intersection identity available for every pair of neighbourhoods.
